---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="Download SP+: SecureProspective" description="Download the current SP+ alpha build. Members only." noindex>
  <Nav />
  <main class="dl-shell" id="main" hidden>
    <header class="dl-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Back Office</p>
      <h1>Download SP+</h1>
      <p class="dl-copy">
        SP+ installs over everything on the machine you put it on. Use a spare computer, or one
        you have already backed up and are ready to erase.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!--
      The alpha warning is a gate, not a banner. A notice nobody has to
      touch is a notice nobody reads, and the specific thing being warned
      about here -- SP+ erases the disk it installs on -- is not recoverable
      once it has happened.
    -->
    <section class="property-card property-card--warn">
      <div class="property-card__band property-card__band--warn">
        <span class="property-card__title">Read this before you download</span>
      </div>
      <div class="property-card__body">
        <p><strong>This is an alpha build. It is unfinished software.</strong></p>
        <ul class="dl-warn-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Installing SP+ erases the entire disk.</strong> Everything on that machine is
            gone: files, photos, other operating systems. There is no undo and no partial install.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Do not install it on the computer you run your practice on.</strong> Use a
            spare machine, or one you have fully backed up and are prepared to lose.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Do not put client data on it.</strong> Not yet. Treat this build as a machine
            you are trying out, not one you are working from.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Expect things to break.</strong> Features will change, and a future build may
            require a clean reinstall rather than an update.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>It comes with no warranty of any kind.</strong> You are installing it at your
            own risk, and SecureProspective is not liable for data loss or downtime.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="dl-note">
          Found something broken? That is the point of an alpha. Tell us at
          <a href="mailto:info@secureprospective.com">info@secureprospective.com</a>.
        </p>
        <label class="dl-ack">
          <input type="checkbox" id="dl-ack" />
          <span>
            I understand this is unfinished software, that installing it will erase the machine
            I put it on, and that I am using a machine I can afford to lose.
          </span>
        </label>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div id="dl-list"></div>

    <!--
      Empty state is a first-class case, not an accident. Between cutting a
      build and publishing it there is a real window where a member signs in
      and finds nothing, and "no releases yet" is a better answer than a
      blank page that reads as broken.
    -->
    <p class="dl-empty" id="dl-empty" hidden>
      There is no published build yet. You will get an email when the first one is ready.
    </p>

    <section class="property-card">
      <div class="property-card__band property-card__band--static">
        <span class="property-card__title">Check what you downloaded</span>
      </div>
      <div class="property-card__body">
        <p>
          Every build has a fingerprint. Run this and compare it to the one shown above. If they
          match, the file arrived intact. If they do not, delete it and download it again.
        </p>
        <p class="dl-label">Windows (PowerShell)</p>
        <pre class="dl-code"><code>Get-FileHash .\FILENAME -Algorithm SHA256</code></pre>
        <p class="dl-label">Windows (Command Prompt)</p>
        <pre class="dl-code"><code>certutil -hashfile FILENAME SHA256</code></pre>
        <p class="dl-label">Mac or Linux</p>
        <pre class="dl-code"><code>shasum -a 256 FILENAME</code></pre>
        <p class="dl-note">
          Replace FILENAME with the file you saved. This is worth doing: it is the whole point of
          publishing the fingerprint.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!--
      Install and after-install documentation. This is written for an advisor
      who has only ever installed software by double-clicking an installer
      inside Windows, and for whom "write the image to a USB stick" is a new
      idea. The passphrase paragraph is the one that matters most: it is the
      only step on this page that can lose a person their data permanently,
      and it happens while they are still deciding whether they trust us.
    -->
    <section class="property-card">
      <div class="property-card__band property-card__band--static">
        <span class="property-card__title">How to install it</span>
      </div>
      <div class="property-card__body">
        <p>
          What you downloaded is a whole operating system, not a program that runs inside Windows.
          It replaces Windows on the machine you install it to. That is the point of it, and it is
          also why the machine has to be one you can afford to erase.
        </p>

        <p class="dl-label">What you need</p>
        <ul class="dl-steps">
          <li>The spare computer you are installing to, plugged into power.</li>
          <li>A USB stick of 8 GB or larger. Writing SP+ to it erases whatever is on it.</li>
          <li>
            A wired network connection if the machine has a port for one. Wireless works, but a
            cable is the difference between an install that finishes and one you have to babysit.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="dl-label">Step by step</p>
        <ol class="dl-steps">
          <li>
            <strong>Write the file to the USB stick.</strong> On Windows, use Rufus or Etcher. On a
            Mac, use Etcher. Both are free, and both do one job: they copy this file onto the stick
            in a form the computer can start from. Copying the file onto the stick the ordinary way
            does not work.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Start the spare machine from the stick.</strong> Put the stick in, turn the
            machine on, and press the key that opens its boot menu while it starts. It is usually
            F12, sometimes F9, F10 or Escape, and the machine normally tells you which for about a
            second. Choose the USB stick from the list.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Work through the installer.</strong> It asks for your language, your time zone,
            the disk to install to, and a name and password for yourself.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Set the disk passphrase when it asks.</strong> Read the next section first.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Let it finish, then remove the stick and restart.</strong> The first start after
            an install takes longer than the ones after it.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="property-card property-card--warn">
      <div class="property-card__band property-card__band--warn">
        <span class="property-card__title">The disk passphrase</span>
      </div>
      <div class="property-card__body">
        <p>
          SP+ encrypts the whole disk. That is not an option you turn on afterwards; it happens
          during the install, and you choose the passphrase that unlocks it. You will type that
          passphrase every time the machine starts, before anything else appears.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Nobody can recover it. Not us, not the manufacturer, not anyone.</strong> There is
          no reset link and no support line that can let you back in. If you lose that passphrase,
          everything on that machine is gone, and the only way forward is to erase the disk and
          install again from the beginning.
        </p>
        <p>
          Write it down before you type it in, and keep the paper somewhere that is not that
          computer. This is the one step on this page that cannot be undone by trying again.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="property-card">
      <div class="property-card__band property-card__band--static">
        <span class="property-card__title">After it is installed</span>
      </div>
      <div class="property-card__body">
        <p>
          The computer updates itself. You do not go looking for updates, you do not buy them, and
          there is nothing to renew. It checks on its own, and when a new version is ready it
          downloads it in the background while you keep working.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the download has finished you will see a notification that says
          <strong>SP+ is ready to update</strong>, with two buttons. <strong>Restart now</strong>
          puts the new version in place immediately and takes a couple of minutes.
          <strong>Later</strong> leaves it where it is.
        </p>
        <p>
          Choosing Later costs you nothing. The update has already been downloaded, and it is
          applied the next time the machine shuts down, so closing the laptop lid at the end of the
          day and shutting it off does the same job as pressing the button. Either way, the next
          time you start it you are on the new version, and you unlock the disk with the same
          passphrase as always.
        </p>
        <p class="dl-note">
          If an update goes wrong, the version you were on before is still on the machine. It is
          kept deliberately, so there is something to put back. You will not be left with a
          computer that does not start.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="dl-actions">
      <a href="/members" class="btn btn--ink">← Back office</a>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<script>
  /*
     Booted from astro:page-load, not from module scope.

     The site runs Astro View Transitions, so a click from another page swaps
     this document in without re-evaluating a module that has already run once.
     Everything below used to execute at module scope, which meant it ran on a
     full page load and never again: arriving here by link left the page in its
     initial state with nothing to move it out of that state, and only a refresh
     fixed it. astro:page-load fires on the first load AND on every client-side
     navigation, which is the only event that matches what this code is for.

     The guard is here because the listener outlives this page. Once registered
     it fires for every navigation in the session, including ones to documents
     that do not contain any of this markup.
  */
  function boot() {
    // #dl-list belongs to this page and to no other.
    if (!document.getElementById('dl-list')) return;

    const shell = document.getElementById('main') as HTMLElement | null;
    const list = document.getElementById('dl-list') as HTMLElement | null;
    const empty = document.getElementById('dl-empty') as HTMLElement | null;

    function humanSize(bytes: number): string {
      if (!bytes) return '';
      const gb = bytes / 1024 / 1024 / 1024;
      if (gb >= 1) return `${gb.toFixed(2)} GB`;
      return `${(bytes / 1024 / 1024).toFixed(0)} MB`;
    }

    const downloadLinks: HTMLAnchorElement[] = [];

    function acknowledged(): boolean {
      const ack = document.getElementById('dl-ack') as HTMLInputElement | null;
      return Boolean(ack?.checked);
    }

    function syncLocks() {
      const ok = acknowledged();
      for (const link of downloadLinks) {
        link.classList.toggle('is-locked', !ok);
        link.setAttribute('aria-disabled', String(!ok));
      }
    }

    function releaseCard(r: any): HTMLElement {
      const card = document.createElement('section');
      card.className = 'property-card';

      const band = document.createElement('div');
      band.className = 'property-card__band property-card__band--static';
      const title = document.createElement('span');
      title.className = 'property-card__title';
      title.textContent = r.label;
      const meta = document.createElement('span');
      meta.className = 'property-card__meta';
      meta.textContent = [humanSize(r.size), r.released].filter(Boolean).join(' · ');
      band.append(title, meta);

      const body = document.createElement('div');
      body.className = 'property-card__body';

      if (r.note) {
        const note = document.createElement('p');
        note.textContent = r.note;
        body.appendChild(note);
      }

      const link = document.createElement('a');
      link.className = 'btn is-locked';
      // aria-disabled, not the disabled attribute: this is an anchor, and
      // `disabled` does nothing on one. The click handler is what actually
      // holds the door -- the attribute is there so a screen reader says so.
      link.setAttribute('aria-disabled', 'true');
      link.addEventListener('click', event => {
        if (!acknowledged()) {
          event.preventDefault();
          const ack = document.getElementById('dl-ack');
          ack?.focus();
          ack?.closest('.property-card')?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'center' });
        }
      });
      downloadLinks.push(link);
      // A plain link, not fetch(). The browser's own download manager handles
      // a 5.7 GB transfer -- it survives a tab close, shows real progress and
      // can resume, none of which a fetch() into memory can do at this size.
      link.href = `/api/download/iso?v=${encodeURIComponent(r.id)}`;
      link.textContent = 'Download';
      body.appendChild(link);

      if (r.sha256) {
        const label = document.createElement('p');
        label.className = 'dl-label';
        label.textContent = 'SHA256';
        const hash = document.createElement('pre');
        hash.className = 'dl-code dl-code--hash';
        const code = document.createElement('code');
        code.textContent = r.sha256;
        hash.appendChild(code);
        body.append(label, hash);
      }

      const fn = document.createElement('p');
      fn.className = 'dl-note';
      fn.textContent = `Saves as ${r.filename}`;
      body.appendChild(fn);

      card.append(band, body);
      return card;
    }

    async function load() {
      try {
        const res = await fetch('/api/download/list');
        if (res.status === 401) {
          window.location.href = '/members/login';
          return;
        }
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(String(res.status));
        const body = await res.json();
        const releases = body.releases ?? [];
        if (list) {
          for (const r of releases) list.appendChild(releaseCard(r));
        }
        syncLocks();
        if (empty) empty.hidden = releases.length > 0;
        if (shell) shell.hidden = false;
      } catch {
        // Failing open to the login page would be wrong here -- the session
        // was fine, the list call was not. Show the page with the honest
        // message instead of bouncing him somewhere he did not ask to go.
        if (empty) {
          empty.textContent =
            'The download list could not be loaded. Refresh the page, and tell us if it keeps happening.';
          empty.hidden = false;
        }
        if (shell) shell.hidden = false;
      }
    }

    document.getElementById('dl-ack')?.addEventListener('change', syncLocks);

    load();
  }

  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', boot);
</script>

<style>
  .dl-shell {
    max-width: 640px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-4) var(--space-7);
  }

  .dl-head {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }

  .dl-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--text-h1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .dl-copy {
    color: var(--fineprint-gray);
  }

  .dl-empty {
    color: var(--fineprint-gray);
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
    background: var(--white);
    padding: var(--space-4);
  }

  .property-card {
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
    background: var(--white);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }

  /* The layout and the white text belong to the band itself. They used to live
     only on --static, so --warn -- which sets an ink background and nothing
     else -- rendered ink text on an ink ground: a 1:1 contrast ratio and a
     heading nobody could read. The modifiers now set the background alone. */
  .property-card__band {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
    color: var(--white);
  }

  .property-card__band--static {
    background: var(--blue-brand);
  }

  .property-card__title {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .property-card__meta {
    font-size: 14px;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  .property-card__body {
    padding: var(--space-4);
  }

  .property-card__body > * + * {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .dl-label {
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .dl-code {
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--white);
    padding: var(--space-3);
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  /* A sha256 is 64 characters with no break opportunity, so it would push
     the card wider than the page on a phone. Wrap it instead of scrolling
     it -- this one is read and compared, not copied as a command. */
  .dl-code--hash code {
    word-break: break-all;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
  }

  .dl-note {
    color: var(--fineprint-gray);
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  /* The install steps are read on a phone, standing next to the machine being
     installed, which is why they are spaced further apart than ordinary body
     copy: losing your place between step 2 and step 3 costs a reboot. */
  .dl-steps {
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
  }

  .dl-steps li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .property-card--warn {
    border-color: var(--ink);
  }

  .property-card__band--warn {
    background: var(--ink);
  }

  .dl-warn-list {
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
  }

  .dl-warn-list li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .dl-ack {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    align-items: flex-start;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--fineprint-gray);
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .dl-ack input {
    margin-top: 4px;
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }

  /* Locked, not hidden. The advisor should be able to see what he is being
     offered and what is standing between him and it. */
  .btn.is-locked {
    opacity: 0.45;
    cursor: not-allowed;
  }

  .dl-actions {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }
</style>
